WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:04.460
- [Narrator] Abandoned
for the last 53 years,

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.190
this eerie whaling station stands remote,

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.850
rusted, but reclaimed by the wildlife

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.123
it once hunted to near extinction.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.344
Welcome to Grytviken.

00:00:15.344 --> 00:00:18.927
(relaxed electronic music)

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.620
Grytviken was a working whaling station

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.410
on the British island of South Georgia,

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:28.330
located 1,500 miles away
from the foot of Argentina.

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.360
Founded in 1904, the vacated settlement

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.110
was once the largest
whaling station in the area.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:39.510
Over 60 years, over 175,000 whales

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:40.990
were killed on the island,

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.560
out of which nine million barrels of oil

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.540
was produced from the
blubber, meat, and bone.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.510
The station closed in 1966,

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.200
after the local whale
populations were decimated.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.240
But there is hope for the
wildlife on this island.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.700
Beyond Grytviken, the island as a whole

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.720
is thought to be the
most important penguin

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.000
and bird breeding area in the world,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.560
hosting over three
million penguins a year.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.380
And the whales are making a comeback.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.040
Although their populations are thought

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.110
not to be fully recovered, the waters

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.150
are now a vibrant whale sanctuary.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.030
The island stands as a testament

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.100
to the natural world's great gift

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.607
to bounce back and reclaim
what was once theirs.

